<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">River Tees</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The River Tees is a river in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, flowing about 137 kilometres east from Cross Fell in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennines">Pennines</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> at Teesside. For much of its length it formed the historic boundary between County Durham and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a>. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it marked the frontier between Bernicia and Deira, and later the southern limit of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>' palatinate of Durham.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/river-tees.jpg" alt="River Tees" width="320" height="239" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nehpetsrob · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:High_Force_waterfall_showing_twin_falls._Teesdale,_England.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
